/*
  protocol/pw_pathway.spthy -- PROTOCOL LAYER, Method 3 (password-based, for a KEYLESS recipient).

  The message is encrypted symmetrically with a fresh passphrase ~pw and put on the network
  (Out(senc(m,pw))) -- the adversary sees the ciphertext, exactly as for a real secure link.
  Everything now rests on the guide's headline caveat: "A password must always travel over a
  channel OTHER than the email." We model the side channel as the private linear fact Oob(...),
  which the Dolev-Yao adversary cannot read; leaking the password means putting it on Out instead.

  The human decision is the SHARE_PW action, answered by the share masks (core/masks share files):
    * Attentive : pw via Oob to the intended recipient        -> secret stays off the wire.
    * Careless  : pw ALSO onto Out (in-band leak)             -> adversary decrypts; recipient still reads
                  (silent compromise).                           (LeakPw)
    * Busy      : pw onto Out to a WRONG recipient, none Oob  -> adversary decrypts AND the intended
                  to the intended one (fat-fingered address)     recipient is locked out. (MisdeliveredPw)

  Both parties are mask-capable here (multi-party goal): after the recipient reads, an adversary
  phishing lure (In) prompts Blake to "confirm" the password on a fake page -- a SECOND SHARE_PW
  request, now for Blake. A degraded recipient re-emits pw onto Out; an Attentive recipient keeps
  it off the wire. So a leak can originate at EITHER end, and both are attributed by the lemmas.

  AlexInit / BlakeKeyless / BlakeHasSecret are linear and consumed once, so the trace is bounded.
*/

// -- sender composes the symmetric ciphertext and poses the SHARE_PW decision --
rule A_compose_pw:
    [ AlexInit(idA), !Keyless('Blake'), !Party('Blake', idB), Fr(~m), Fr(~pw), Fr(~rid) ]
  --[ Branch('Alex','PW'), Send('Alex','PW', ~m), Secret(~m),
      Request('Alex', ~rid, 'SHARE_PW') ]->
    [ Out(senc(~m, ~pw)), Posted('Alex','PW', ~m),
      !Req('Alex', ~rid, 'SHARE_PW', <idB, ~pw>, 'Easy'),
      // Layer-1 objective step (agnostic interface): composing the encrypted message under a
      // deadline is a 'compute' action -- the SAME tag the KDF ceremony emits, so the one
      // migrated sigma3 (time_pressure) fires here too, with no 'SHARE_PW' task name in the detector.
      !Step('Alex', ~rid, 'compute') ]

// The recipient read + recipient-side phishing OUTCOMES are shared with profile S1 and live in
// protocol/deliver.spthy (B_read_pw / B_phish_prompt). This file is just the sender's compose producer.
